@startuml c4-dynamic-recall
!include ../vendor/style.puml

title Dynamic view — recall investigation, from authorised query to evidence export

autonumber
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 210
skinparam BoxPadding 8
skinparam participant {
  BackgroundColor #438DD5
  FontColor #FFFFFF
  BorderColor #2E6DA4
}
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #444444
  LifeLineBorderColor #999999
  GroupBorderColor #999999
  GroupHeaderFontColor #444444
}

participant "Authorised investigator\n(retailer or regulator)" as officer
participant "Traceability App\n(Recall module / QueryPort)" as app
participant "Operational PostgreSQL\n(QueryStore, lineage, proof records)" as db

== Phase 1 · Authorise and fix the query scope ==
officer -> app: Investigate lot L with acting organisation and role
app -> app: Check access scope; fix data watermark W

== Phase 2 · Trace and verify recorded evidence ==
app -> db: Scoped lineage, source events and confirmation metadata at W
db --> app: Events, edges, signatures and locally retained proof material
app -> app: Traverse upstream/downstream within the bound;\nverify available digests, signatures and inclusion proofs\nagainst retained finalised commitments
note right of app: Preserve competing claims. Keep confirmed and\nprovisional impacts distinct. Missing proof, stale data\nor a traversal limit is reported as incomplete/degraded;\nit never implies that no affected lot exists.
app --> officer: Potentially affected lots and provenance;\nwatermark, confirmation/verification status and coverage limits

== Phase 3 · Export scoped evidence ==
officer -> app: Export the investigated scope at W
app -> app: Recheck export permission; apply field allowlist
app -> db: Audit actor, scope, watermark and export reference
app --> officer: Scoped records and verification material;\nexclude owner-held private document contents

note over officer, db: Investigators can independently check exported proofs against public-chain commitments.\nRecall queries use local materialisations; backup/chain calls are not on the synchronous read path.\nFormal notices, private-document exchange and physical recall execution use existing external processes.

@enduml
